---
import Hero from "../components/Hero.astro";
import { PromptBlock } from "../components/PromptBlock";
import { StepNav } from "../components/StepNav";
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { PROMPTS, REPO_URL, SITE } from "../lib/site";
---

<BaseLayout>
	<Hero slot="hero" />
	<StepNav slot="nav" client:load />

	<section class="mb-12 grid gap-4">
		<p class="lede">
			{SITE.description}
		</p>
		<p class="lede">
			One prompt per step. Your agent writes the config and opens the pull request; you click the
			buttons and watch the isolation happen.
		</p>
	</section>

	{/* ─── Step 0 ─── */}
	<section id="prereqs" class="step">
		<header class="step-header">
			<p class="step-meta">Step 0 · before you start</p>
			<h2 class="step-title">Before you start</h2>
		</header>

		<div class="step-body">
			<p>You need:</p>
			<ul>
				<li><strong class="text-kumo-default">Node.js</strong>, <code>git</code>, and a terminal</li>
				<li>
					<strong class="text-kumo-default">A coding agent</strong> — whatever you already use.
					<a href="https://opencode.ai">OpenCode</a> works fine if you don't have one.
				</li>
				<li>
					A <strong class="text-kumo-default">GitHub account</strong> — the deploy button creates a
					repo in it.
				</li>
				<li>
					A <strong class="text-kumo-default">free Cloudflare account</strong> — no credit card
					required. <a href="https://dash.cloudflare.com/sign-up">Sign up</a> now if you don't have
					one; it's one less thing to do mid-step.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong class="text-kumo-default">Optional but helpful:</strong> the
					<a href="https://cli.github.com/">GitHub CLI</a> (<code>gh</code>), authenticated —
					lets your agent open the pull request for you in Step 3.
				</li>
			</ul>
		</div>
	</section>

	{/* ─── Step 1 ─── */}
	<section id="deploy" class="step">
		<header class="step-header">
			<p class="step-meta">Step 1 · ~5 min</p>
			<h2 class="step-title">Deploy to Cloudflare</h2>
			<p class="step-lede">
				One click. A repo in your account, a database, and a production Worker — wired up for you.
			</p>
		</header>

		<div class="step-body mb-6">
			<p>
				Click the button below. It creates a copy of the app's repo in your own GitHub account,
				provisions a production D1 database, connects <strong class="text-kumo-default"
					>Workers Builds</strong
				> (Cloudflare's own CI/CD) to that repo, and deploys.
			</p>
			<p>No custom GitHub Actions to set up, no secrets to paste in. That part is automatic.</p>
		</div>

		<div class="mb-6">
			<a href={`https://deploy.workers.cloudflare.com/?url=${REPO_URL}`}>
				<img src="https://deploy.workers.cloudflare.com/button" alt="Deploy to Cloudflare" />
			</a>
		</div>

		<div class="callout callout--check">
			<span class="callout-icon" aria-hidden="true">✓</span>
			<div>
				<p class="callout-title">Checkpoint</p>
				<p class="callout-body">
					Open the production URL Cloudflare gives you. You should see an <strong>Activity Log</strong
					> with three seeded entries — <em>"Deployed the production Worker"</em>,
					<em>"Provisioned the D1 database"</em>, <em>"Wired up Workers Builds"</em>. Remember
					what's in there; you'll compare it against your Preview shortly.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="mt-6 arch" aria-label="Architecture after step 1">
			<div class="arch-row">
				<span class="arch-node arch-node--accent">Worker</span>
				<span class="arch-arrow">↔</span>
				<span class="arch-node arch-node--accent">D1 · production</span>
			</div>
			<div class="arch-row">
				<span class="arch-node">*.workers.dev</span>
				<span class="arch-arrow">→</span>
				<span class="arch-node arch-node--muted">Internet</span>
			</div>
			<p class="arch-caption">One Worker, one production D1 database, seeded with three rows.</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	{/* ─── Step 2 ─── */}
	<section id="isolate" class="step">
		<header class="step-header">
			<p class="step-meta">Step 2 · ~6 min</p>
			<h2 class="step-title">Isolate a resource</h2>
			<p class="step-lede">
				A new branch inherits your code automatically. It does not inherit your database.
			</p>
		</header>

		<div class="step-body mb-6">
			<p>
				Cloudflare Workers Previews build a live deployment for every pull request — same code,
				same routes, same bindings, <strong class="text-kumo-default">by default</strong>. For most
				resources that's exactly what you want.
			</p>
			<p>
				D1 is the exception, on purpose: Cloudflare will not point a Preview at your production
				database unless you explicitly tell it to, so a stray branch can't accidentally read or
				write real data. You opt in with a <code>previews.d1_databases</code> override in
				<code>wrangler.json</code>.
			</p>
			<p>
				Ask your agent to create a branch, provision a brand-new D1 database, and add that
				override:
			</p>
		</div>

		<PromptBlock label="Step 2 · Isolate a resource" text={PROMPTS.isolateResource} />

		<div class="mt-5 callout callout--check">
			<span class="callout-icon" aria-hidden="true">✓</span>
			<div>
				<p class="callout-title">Checkpoint</p>
				<p class="callout-body">
					<code>wrangler.json</code> now has a <code>previews.d1_databases</code> block pointing at
					a database that isn't the production one, and the change is committed.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="mt-4 callout callout--aha">
			<span class="callout-icon" aria-hidden="true">!</span>
			<div>
				<p class="callout-title">Aha</p>
				<p class="callout-body">
					Durable Objects and Containers don't need this step — they isolate per Preview
					automatically, no override block required. D1 (and R2, and KV) need one because they're
					shared, standalone resources that outlive any single Worker; the platform can't safely
					guess which copy a branch should get.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	{/* ─── Step 3 ─── */}
	<section id="preview" class="step">
		<header class="step-header">
			<p class="step-meta">Step 3 · ~5 min</p>
			<h2 class="step-title">Open your Preview</h2>
			<p class="step-lede">Push the branch, open a pull request, and Workers Builds does the rest.</p>
		</header>

		<div class="step-body mb-6">
			<p>Ask your agent to push and open the PR:</p>
		</div>

		<PromptBlock label="Step 3 · Open a pull request" text={PROMPTS.openPullRequest} />

		<div class="mt-6 callout callout--warn">
			<span class="callout-icon" aria-hidden="true">!</span>
			<div>
				<p class="callout-title">Don't wait on the PR comment</p>
				<p class="callout-body">
					Cloudflare's bot usually comments the Preview URL on the PR within a minute or two, but
					it can occasionally lag well behind the deploy itself. If it hasn't shown up yet, check
					the <strong>Workers Builds</strong> check on the PR — once that's green, your Preview is
					live even if the comment isn't there yet.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="mt-5 callout callout--check">
			<span class="callout-icon" aria-hidden="true">✓</span>
			<div>
				<p class="callout-title">Checkpoint</p>
				<p class="callout-body">
					Open the Preview URL. You should see an <strong>empty</strong> Activity Log — no seeded
					rows. That's your new database, freshly created, genuinely isolated from production.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="mt-6 arch" aria-label="Architecture after step 3">
			<div class="arch-row">
				<span class="arch-node arch-node--accent">Worker · main</span>
				<span class="arch-arrow">↔</span>
				<span class="arch-node arch-node--accent">D1 · production (3 rows)</span>
			</div>
			<div class="arch-row">
				<span class="arch-node arch-node--accent">Worker · your branch (Preview)</span>
				<span class="arch-arrow">↔</span>
				<span class="arch-node arch-node--accent">D1 · your branch (0 rows)</span>
			</div>
			<p class="arch-caption">
				Same code deployed twice. Two separate databases, because you told it to.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	{/* ─── Step 4 ─── */}
	<section id="interact" class="step">
		<header class="step-header">
			<p class="step-meta">Step 4 · ~5 min</p>
			<h2 class="step-title">Prove isolation</h2>
			<p class="step-lede">Don't take the empty list on faith. Write to it, then check production.</p>
		</header>

		<div class="step-body mb-6">
			<ol>
				<li>On your Preview URL, type something into <strong>Log an activity…</strong> and click <strong>Add entry</strong>.</li>
				<li>Add one more. Then click <strong>Delete</strong> on one of them.</li>
				<li>Open your <strong>production</strong> URL from Step 1 in another tab.</li>
			</ol>
		</div>

		<div class="callout callout--check">
			<span class="callout-icon" aria-hidden="true">✓</span>
			<div>
				<p class="callout-title">Checkpoint</p>
				<p class="callout-body">
					Production still shows exactly the same three original rows — nothing you added or
					deleted in the Preview shows up there. Two deployments of identical code, writing to two
					different databases.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="mt-4 callout callout--aha">
			<span class="callout-icon" aria-hidden="true">!</span>
			<div>
				<p class="callout-title">Aha</p>
				<p class="callout-body">
					This is what "isolated environment for every change" actually means in practice — not a
					slide, a database you can watch stay separate while you poke at it.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	{/* ─── Step 5 ─── */}
	<section id="observability" class="step">
		<header class="step-header">
			<p class="step-meta">Step 5 · ~4 min</p>
			<h2 class="step-title">Observability</h2>
			<p class="step-lede">Your Preview has its own logs and traces too — not just its own database.</p>
		</header>

		<div class="step-body mb-6">
			<p>In the Cloudflare dashboard:</p>
			<ol>
				<li>Go to <strong>Workers &amp; Pages</strong> → your Worker.</li>
				<li>
					Open the <strong>Observability</strong> tab while on the Worker's <strong>production</strong
					> view, then switch to the <strong>Previews</strong> tab and select your branch.
				</li>
				<li>Refresh your Preview URL a couple of times, then check the request list.</li>
			</ol>
		</div>

		<div class="callout callout--check">
			<span class="callout-icon" aria-hidden="true">✓</span>
			<div>
				<p class="callout-title">Checkpoint</p>
				<p class="callout-body">
					You see requests and logs scoped to your branch's Preview — separate from production's
					traffic, same as the database.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	{/* ─── Step 6 ─── */}
	<section id="final" class="step">
		<header class="step-header">
			<p class="step-meta">Step 6 · TBD</p>
			<h2 class="step-title">TBD — pending a conversation with Yomna</h2>
		</header>

		<div class="callout callout--warn">
			<span class="callout-icon" aria-hidden="true">?</span>
			<div>
				<p class="callout-title">Not decided yet</p>
				<p class="callout-body">
					This is a genuine placeholder, not a mistake — the final step of the workshop is still
					being worked out with Yomna (PM). Don't build content into this slot until that's
					settled.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	{/* ─── Wrap-up ─── */}
	<section id="wrap-up" class="step">
		<header class="step-header">
			<p class="step-meta">After the session</p>
			<h2 class="step-title">Clean up</h2>
		</header>

		<div class="step-body">
			<p>
				Closing or merging your pull request deletes the Preview deployment automatically. It does
				<strong class="text-kumo-default">not</strong> delete the D1 database you created in Step 2
				— that's a standalone account resource that outlives the branch. Delete it yourself once
				you're done:
			</p>
		</div>

		<div class="command-block mt-5">
			<div class="command-block-bar">
				<span class="command-block-label">Cleanup</span>
			</div>
			<pre><code>npx wrangler d1 delete &lt;your-preview-db-name&gt;</code></pre>
		</div>
	</section>
</BaseLayout>
